chromium-embedded-framework for Debian
======================================

This package uses Multiple Upstream Tarballs (MUT) format with 3.0 (quilt)
source format. All source components are distributed as separate tarballs.

================================================================================
SOURCE PACKAGE STRUCTURE (MUT)
================================================================================

The source package consists of 3 tarballs:

    chromium-embedded-framework_VERSION.orig.tar.xz
        CEF source code (main upstream tarball)

    chromium-embedded-framework_VERSION.orig-chromium.tar.xz
        Chromium browser source (from Debian chromium package)

    chromium-embedded-framework_VERSION.orig-depot-tools.tar.xz
        Google's depot_tools build system

When extracted with dpkg-source -x, these create:

    chromium-embedded-framework-VERSION/
    ├── cef/                    <- CEF source (from orig.tar.xz)
    ├── chromium/               <- From orig-chromium.tar.xz
    ├── depot-tools/            <- From orig-depot-tools.tar.xz (note: hyphen)
    └── debian/                 <- Packaging files

NOTE: MUT component names use HYPHENS (depot-tools), not underscores.
The main tarball contains CEF source in a cef/ subdirectory.

================================================================================
BUILD INSTRUCTIONS
================================================================================

Standard Build (from source package):

    $ dpkg-source -x chromium-embedded-framework_*.dsc
    $ cd chromium-embedded-framework-*/
    $ dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc

The MUT components are automatically extracted by dpkg-source -x.

Build Time: ~2-7 hours (depends on hardware; 2h on modern 24-core, 7h+ on older systems)
Disk Space: ~40 GB required

Obtaining Source Tarballs:

Once the package is in the Debian archive, use:

    $ apt-get source chromium-embedded-framework

Or download with dget:

    $ dget https://deb.debian.org/debian/pool/.../chromium-embedded-framework_VERSION.dsc

To recreate tarballs from upstream sources (e.g., for initial packaging):

    $ ./debian/scripts/get-orig-tarballs.sh [VERSION] [CEF_BRANCH]

This script downloads CEF from Bitbucket, Chromium from Debian's archive,
and depot-tools from Google, then creates properly named MUT tarballs.

================================================================================
COMPONENT TARBALLS
================================================================================

1. CEF Source (orig.tar.xz)
---------------------------
Obtained from: https://bitbucket.org/chromiumembedded/cef
Branch: Must match Chromium's build number (e.g., branch 7499 for Chromium 143.x)

2. Chromium Source (orig-chromium.tar.xz)
-----------------------------------------
Obtained from: Debian chromium package source
Command: apt-get source --download-only chromium
Rename: mv chromium_*.orig.tar.xz chromium-embedded-framework_VERSION.orig-chromium.tar.xz

NOTE: This tarball contains vanilla Chromium source. The Debian Chromium patches
(from chromium_*.debian.tar.xz) are applied during the build in Step 5.5 of
debian/rules. This ensures CEF builds with the same system integration patches
that Debian Chromium uses (libc++, system libraries, build fixes, etc.).

3. depot-tools (orig-depot-tools.tar.xz)
----------------------------------------
Obtained from: https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/tools/depot_tools.git
This tarball is cleaned of binary files (.cipd*, *.exe, *.dll, etc.)

4. Rust Standard Library (system rust-src)
------------------------------------------
The build uses system rust-src package (Build-Depends: rust-src).

Debian rust-src 1.91.1+ provides all files required by Chromium's build system,
including sync/nonpoison/condvar.rs and rwlock.rs that were missing in earlier
versions. No bundled Rust tarball is needed.

================================================================================
DESIGN DECISIONS
================================================================================

1. WHY FULL CHROMIUM SOURCE (not just headers)
----------------------------------------------
CEF uses internal Chromium APIs, not just the public API. For example:

- CEF includes 43+ headers from content/browser/ (internal implementation)
  rather than content/public/browser/ (stable public API)
- libcef/browser/browser_host_base.cc includes internal headers like
  content/browser/renderer_host/render_frame_host_impl.h
- CEF's off-screen rendering extends content::RenderWidgetHostViewBase
  from content/browser/renderer_host/render_widget_host_view_base.h

These internal classes don't have stable ABIs and their implementation can
change between Chromium versions. CEF hooks into Chromium's internal browser
infrastructure, requiring source-level integration with a matching Chromium
version. A headers-only approach (chromium-headers package) would not work
because the ninja build targets depend on compiled Chromium object files.

2. WHY DEPOT_TOOLS IS BUNDLED
-----------------------------
depot_tools is Google's build infrastructure for Chromium-based projects.
CEF's automate-git.py script expects depot_tools for:
- gclient: Dependency management and configuration
- gn: Build file generation
- autoninja: Parallel compilation wrapper

NETWORK ISOLATION: This package does NOT access the internet during build.
- Patch 0003-network-isolation-disable-google-storage-downloads makes
  download_from_google_storage.py a no-op
- The --no-depot-tools-update flag prevents upstream pulls
- All source is bundled in the MUT tarballs

The git clone commands in debian/rules error messages only explain how to
obtain depot_tools if missing - they don't execute during normal builds.

3. LOCAL GIT INITIALIZATION
---------------------------
debian/rules creates local git repositories with git init && git commit.
This satisfies CEF's automate-git.py which expects git repositories for
source directories. This is completely network-free but unusual for Debian.

A cleaner approach would be patching automate-git.py to remove git
requirements entirely, though the necessary changes would be complex
for limited benefit.

================================================================================
CONFIGURATION DECISIONS
================================================================================

1. RAM-BASED PARALLEL JOB LIMITING
----------------------------------
CEF/Chromium compilation requires ~3GB RAM per parallel job. The build system
automatically limits ninja parallelism based on available RAM to prevent OOM
killer from terminating the build.

Implementation in debian/rules:
- Respects DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS=parallel=N if set (never increases beyond it)
- Calculates safe jobs based on available RAM (~3GB per job)
- Uses minimum of requested and RAM-safe values
- Considers cgroup memory limits for containerized builds (buildds)

Example behavior:
| Machine         | RAM   | CPUs | DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS | Resulting Jobs |
|-----------------|-------|------|-------------------|----------------|
| Dev workstation | 64GB  | 24   | (none)            | 21 (64/3)      |
| Dev workstation | 64GB  | 24   | parallel=8        | 8              |
| Small buildd    | 16GB  | 8    | parallel=8        | 5 (16/3)       |
| Large buildd    | 128GB | 64   | parallel=64       | 42 (128/3)     |

This follows established Debian patterns:
- llvm-toolchain-19: https://sources.debian.org/src/llvm-toolchain-19/1:19.1.4-1/debian/rules/#L92
- polymake (4GB/job): https://sources.debian.org/src/polymake/4.12-3/debian/rules/?hl=15#L15
- Pending debhelper MR: https://salsa.debian.org/debian/debhelper/-/merge_requests/128

2. DISTRIBUTION CONFIGURATION (--minimal-distrib-only)
------------------------------------------------------
CEF's automate-git.py can create multiple distribution types (standard, minimal,
client, sandbox, tools). This package uses --minimal-distrib-only to create
only the minimal distribution.

Why minimal-only:
- Creates only 1 distribution command (vs 2+ with --minimal-distrib)
- Avoids parallel execution of make_distrib.py processes
- Skips doxygen documentation generation (saves 1-2GB memory)
- Prevents OOM during distribution phase on memory-constrained systems
- Minimal distribution contains everything needed for Debian packages:
  * libcef.so (Release build)
  * Headers (include/)
  * CMake configuration files
  * Resources (PAK files, locales, ICU data)
  * ANGLE libraries (libEGL.so, libGLESv2.so)

What minimal excludes (not needed for Debian packages):
- Debug build (libcef.so with debug symbols - we use dh_strip instead)
- Test applications (cefclient, ceftests)
- Doxygen documentation

IMPORTANT: The --minimal-distrib flag (without -only) creates BOTH standard
and minimal distributions, which can trigger parallel execution and OOM.
Always use --minimal-distrib-only for single-distribution builds.

3. SYSTEM LIBC++ (use_custom_libcxx=false)
------------------------------------------
Uses Debian's libc++-19-dev instead of bundled libc++.
Aligns with Debian Chromium's approach.

4. SYSTEM LLVM/CLANG (clang-19)
-------------------------------
Uses Debian's clang-19 toolchain. No bundled compiler.

5. SYSTEM FFMPEG (is_component_ffmpeg=true)
-------------------------------------------
Uses system FFmpeg for media codecs.
Enables H.264/AAC when user installs libavcodec-extra.

================================================================================
PATCHES
================================================================================

CEF Patches (debian/patches/*.cef.patch)
----------------------------------------
Applied via quilt during dpkg-source extraction.
Patch paths have cef/ prefix (e.g., cef/tools/automate/automate-git.py).
See debian/patches/series for the order.

Key patches:
- 0017-sequential-distribution-commands.cef.patch: Forces sequential execution
  of make_distrib.py even when multiple distribution types are requested.
  This prevents OOM by avoiding parallel file copying operations. With
  --minimal-distrib-only this patch is redundant but provides a safety net.

- 0018-disable-distrib-clang-format.cef.patch: Disables clang-format during
  distribution phase. See "CLANG-FORMAT AND MUT BUILDS" section below.

Chromium Patches (debian/patches/chromium/*.chromium.patch)
-----------------------------------------------------------
Applied manually via patch -p1 in debian/rules.
These patches modify Chromium source, not CEF source.

================================================================================
CLANG-FORMAT AND MUT BUILDS
================================================================================

The distribution phase (make_distrib.py) formats C/C++ headers using clang-format.
This causes problems specific to MUT tarball builds that don't occur with git-cloned
depot_tools.

The Problem:
-----------
When make_distrib.py copies header files, it calls clang_format_inplace() for each
C/C++ file (~16,000 headers). This resolves to depot-tools/clang_format.py, a Python
wrapper that:

1. Tries FindClangFormatToolInChromiumTree() - searches for bundled clang-format
2. Calls gclient_paths.GetPrimarySolutionPath() - searches for .gclient file
3. Runs 'git rev-parse --show-toplevel' subprocess when .gclient not found
4. Falls back to FindClangFormatInPath() - searches PATH for clang-format

Each header file spawns a NEW Python process with its own empty LRU cache, so:
- 16,000+ Python processes (~20MB each)
- 16,000+ git subprocesses
- ~320GB virtual memory demand → OOM killer triggered

Why MUT Builds Are Affected:
---------------------------
Git-cloned depot_tools (pre-MUT):
- Contains .cipd_bin/ with downloaded clang-format binary
- Has cached state from previous gclient runs
- clang_format.py finds the binary quickly

Tarball-extracted depot-tools (MUT):
- Clean extraction without .cipd/ or .cipd_bin/
- No downloaded binaries (tarballs are static)
- clang_format.py takes the slow search path for every file

Why Not Just Add clang-format Build-Dep:
---------------------------------------
Even with system clang-format installed, the slow path still executes first:
1. Each process still calls FindClangFormatToolInChromiumTree()
2. Still runs gclient_paths searches and git subprocesses
3. Eventually finds /usr/bin/clang-format and succeeds
4. But still spawns 16,000+ Python processes with git subprocesses

The Solution:
------------
Patch 0018 adds --no-format to the make_distrib.py invocation, which:
- Skips clang_format_inplace() entirely
- Zero Python wrapper processes spawned
- Zero git subprocesses
- Distribution completes in seconds instead of OOM

This is safe because:
- Headers are already properly formatted by upstream CEF
- Distribution headers are read-only for end users
- clang-format is purely cosmetic, doesn't affect functionality

================================================================================
MAINTENANCE
================================================================================

Updating CEF Version:
1. Update CEF source in orig.tar.xz
2. Update CEF_BRANCH in debian/rules
3. Update chromium version to match CEF requirements
4. Regenerate orig-chromium.tar.xz from new Debian chromium
5. Test build and fix any patch conflicts

Updating Chromium Version:
1. apt-get source --download-only chromium
2. Rename and replace orig-chromium.tar.xz
3. Test build and fix patch conflicts

Updating LLVM Version:
1. Update Build-Depends in debian/control
2. Update CLANG_MVERS in debian/rules
3. Update patch 0012-bindgen-libclang-abspath.chromium.patch

================================================================================
FUTURE MIGRATION (When chromium-source package exists)
================================================================================

When the Debian Chromium team provides a chromium-source binary package:

Migration Steps:
1. Remove `orig-chromium.tar.xz` from MUT tarballs
2. Add `Build-Depends: chromium-source` to debian/control
3. Modify Step 5 in debian/rules to copy from /usr/src/chromium/ (or wherever
   the chromium-source package installs the source)
4. Adjust Step 5.5 based on the source package format (see below)

Handling Different Source Formats:
---------------------------------

The chromium-source package may provide source in two formats:

A) VANILLA SOURCE (pristine upstream):
   - Keep Step 5.5 to apply Debian Chromium patches during build
   - Extract patches from the chromium-source package or debian.tar.xz
   - This is identical to our current approach

B) PRE-PATCHED SOURCE (Debian patches already applied):
   - Remove or disable Step 5.5 (patches already in source)
   - Update this documentation to note patches are pre-applied
   - Verify all expected patches are included

Detection: Check the chromium-source package documentation or test by:
   grep -l "libcxx" /usr/src/chromium/build/config/BUILDCONFIG.gn
   - If found: source is VANILLA (needs Step 5.5)
   - If not found: source is PRE-PATCHED (skip Step 5.5)

Why Migration is Straightforward:
---------------------------------
- Our Step 5.5 patch infrastructure handles both scenarios
- All Debian Chromium patches are applied visibly during build
- debian/rules already handles system library integration
- No hidden modifications in our tarballs

